The matched null is behaving pathologically under the cost grid (NVDA null segment median +1.134, null dispersion 0.558; TSLA similar) and is non-monotonic in cost. That is a red flag. Let me check the engine's slippage semantics in the tests before drawing any conclusion.
[0m→ [0mRead tests/test_engine.py[90m [limit=110, offset=30][0m
The slippage convention is intentional per `tests/test_engine.py` ("short fills must also price at close + slippage") — all fills price at close + slippage regardless of side. But that still cannot explain a null median swinging from −0.108 to +1.134 for a *fixed* coin-flip signal across cost levels (a swing of +1.24 log return). I must empirically locate the source before trusting the artifact. Writing a focused diagnostic.
